india's prime minister narendra modi is  in berlin today where he's a meeting  with german chancellor olaf schutz while  the focus of modi's visit is on trade  and energy partnerships the war in  ukraine is overshadowing the meeting  india which imports much of its military  hardware from russia is trying to walk a  diplomatic tightrope between moscow and  the west modi has so far refused to  condemn the russian invasion or to  impose sanctions on russia straining  india's relationship with the eu  minister of india days ago in delhi  prime minister narendra modi opens  india's flagship global forum the  reizina dialogue  guest of honor ozola fonda line  president of the european commission her  message europe and india must grow  closer together  for the european union strengthening  and energizing  its partnership with india is a priority  in this upcoming decade and therefore i  am very pleased that today prime  minister modi and i have agreed to  establish an eu india trade and  technology council to tackle  key trade economic and technological  challenges  but the war raging thousands of  kilometers away in ukraine is casting a  shadow on europe's ties with india  prime minister narendra modi has not  condemned the russian invasion he's  trying not to risk india's traditionally  close ties with russia  vonderlein spoke emotionally of her own  visit to ukraine where she witnessed the  mass graves of butcher and she insisted  that india could not look away  the outcome of the war  will not only determine the future of  europe  but also deeply affect  the indo-pacific region and the rest of  the world  for the indo-pacific region it is as  important as for europe  that borders are respected  and that spheres of influence are  rejected  but for india's foreign minister some of  this message rings hollow  we've been hearing for the last two  months a lot of our lot of arguments  from europe saying you know that things  happening in europe and asia should  worry about it because these could  happen in asia  guess what things have been happening in  asia for the last 10 years  a prime example for indian critics of  europe the chaotic western withdrawal  last year from afghanistan raising  questions over the west's commitment to  the region into its values  now let's bring in dw's chief  international editor richard walker is  here in berlin ntw correspondent monira  chowdhury in delhi uh richard let's  start with you how is schultz trying to  bind india closer to the west  yeah well gerhard we saw some examples  of what's going on in the relationship  between europe as a whole and india uh  uh in the report just there uh from  delhi last week with ursula fonda line  announcing this big tech and trade  council uh uh to to boost uh uh to boost  uh kind of discussions between the two  sides coordinate coordination between  the two sides on the crucial areas of  technology and trade in the ways that  they intersect and you have similar kind  of discussions going on here with  germany also setting up various  different types of agreements to try to  increase the engagement with india  because what you have here  in germany and europe as a whole is a  recognition of the importance of india a  growing recognition of the importance of  india strategically in the region being  discussed as the indo-pacific these days  not so much asia pacific but with india  very much in the heart of that  and that is not just because india is  rising it's been rising for years but  it's also because of a growing  recognition of the challenge that china  is presenting across that region and the  sense that trying to bind india closer  to the west is a crucial way of trying  to stand up to that challenge  let's go to manira in delhi there manira  how do people in india react to the the  war in ukraine what's public opinion  like is it behind modi and its policies  well when it comes to the diplomatic  response of india we all know what it's  been like over the past uh two months  since the invasion began the the  diplomatic response by the indian  government has also evolved but is still  far away from openly condemning the  invasion when it comes internally to the  public to the public opinion well there  are both sides there are both sets of  people there both sets of responses  there are those people who are  supporting russia who are saying well  what's happening with the ukrainians is  unfortunate uh but they are supportive  of russia saying that what they call the  western supremacy in a way needs to be  challenged and what russia is doing is  well within its rights to do so but at  the same time there is a very vocal  lobby of public here in india who are  very vocally and openly critical of the  invasion and not only of the invasion  but of the indian government's  diplomatic response saying that there's  no need to walk the tight rope that  india has been doing  so  public opinion being one thing when ira  what about  the business interests there are indian  companies willing to join the west in  moving away from russia economically or  do they now see a chance to establish a  foothold in the russian market  well if we talk about the responses from  the business industries of course it has  not been as enthusiastic and severing  its ties as we can see in the west  although two big companies have  announced starting their business with  russia one of them is infosys which is a  software giant the other is an indian  multinational steel making company  called tata steel both of them have  joined a handful of indian companies who  have announced severing of ties with  russia but as you just said this is also  opening up an opportunity for other  indian companies to as you said gaining  a foothold in the russian market and  basically taking advantage of the vacuum  that has been created as other  global companies are making their way  out of russia so that of course is  happening but that of course is not an  easy proposition and at the same time  after being shunned uh by the u.s and  the european union there is a supply hit  in russia as well and it is looking  towards india to uh basically match some  of those uh supply hits especially when  it comes to pharmaceuticals and textiles  and agriculture  richard walker international  correspondent back in here in berlin um  we've heard that the indian european  relations are under strain right now do  you think this visit uh will  help to bridge this the rift if you will  well yeah i mean i think it  it's not necessarily something that we  can talk of as a massive rift because  you're not going to hear a huge amount  of really open criticism  from the german side of india i think  you see both the europeans and the  americans  being very cautious in criticizing india  we just heard in the report there uh  foreign minister jay shankar what his  response was when he talked about well  guess what things have been going on in  asia in recent years uh europe perhaps  wasn't as interested in us then as it  should have been but certainly you hear  a lot from german politicians of trying  to sort of pull india in rather than  push it away to try to draw it into uh  into closer ties with the west through  all of these agreements and try to kind  of coax it in a direction that they  would like to see but there is one other  elephant in the room here and that is  the current political situation within  india manira can of course talk more  about that but uh there is a sense and i  was in india last week reporting there's  a sense of a really agitated environment  in india's society  narendra modi's hindu nationalist  government is accused of trampling on  the rights of the muslim minority there  there have been uh  incidents of hate demolitions of of  muslim homes and businesses  a really quite febrile atmosphere and  this is another thing which is which is  certainly causing concern in germany  that they want to cooperate with the  democratic india but they're one  worrying about what direction democratic  india is actually taking  dws chief international editor richard  walker there and our correspondent maria  chowri in delhi thank you both  germany is one of india's top 10 global  trading partners  more than 1700 german companies are  present there accounting for about four  hundred thousand jobs in the indian  labor market  i'm joined now by amrita narlikar she's  president of the giga institute in  hamburg i'm rita thank you for joining  us you've written that the eu and india  are natural allies and yet trade  relations between the two have been  difficult in recent years why is that  uh hi stephen great to see you  so um yes what i've written is that the  eu and india are even though they should  be natural allies they are involved in a  rather clumsy tangle  and there are several reasons for this  a big one is that the eu tends to place  a lot of emphasis on business and  development relations with india  and this isn't these are issues that  india too is interested in promoting  especially under the business and growth  oriented modi government but the eu then  very swiftly goes down  a cul-de-sac of the narrowly defined  values  of social and environmental standards  whenever the fta is discussed  and the tone of these discussions can  come across  as if the europeans are preaching to  their indian counterparts  on the importance of liberal values  and neither the content nor the tone of  these discussions are useful  for winning trust especially when  negotiating with an ancient civilization  like in  well to play devil's advocate here these  big trade packages are very complicated  to get approved by all the countries in  the eu and there often is a focus on  social and environmental uh the  practices  of the trade partner isn't it likely  that countries like france like germany  like belgium are going to look at those  issues when it comes to a trade package  with india and that could be a problem  to do one of these overarching packages  well what i would emphasize is that the  overarching packages are important and  needed  the notion of values however needs to be  expanded  to include  environmental standards social standards  but also think about geo strategic  questions questions of security  and macro level values which include  democracy liberalism pluralism  uh these are all values that india  shares with the european union the  problem is that trade is regarded quite  narrowly um by the eu by the commission  and it and that is why we get this  emphasis much more on the narrower  uh labor and environmental standards  rather than bigger questions of  democracy pluralism  in the sense is it likely that the war  in ukraine will complicate things  between the two sides or could it  actually represent an opportunity  if the war uh the russian invasion of  ukraine could uh  it could be a really important trigger  for deepening the cooperation between eu  and india and also germany and india  um so on the one hand you could keep  trying to point the finger at india for  not taking a strong stance in favor of  democratic ukraine  and this will not help eu india or in or  indo-german cooperation but in fact what  the what  what the war has shown germany and  europe  is the risks of over dependence on  russia  india also has a difficult neighbor in  its region china  and let's not forget the no limits  partnership announced by russia and  china earlier this year so there is an  authoritarian advance happening on the  borders of the eu and happening on the  borders of india  and  so this is an amazingly important  opportunity and necessity for both india  and germany to be building secure supply  chains for eu and india to be building  secure supply chains  especially in strategically important  sectors like 5g technology medical  supplies  infrastructure  also dual use technology  all right i'm rita narlikar's president  of the giga institute in hamburg talking  about india eu trade relations i'm rita  thank you very much

